<p>
  Finding a new customer takes messages, time and often a discount. Selling to
  somebody who already bought from you takes one text. That gap is the reason
  most established businesses make the majority of their money from people who
  have bought before.
</p>

<p>
  At your size this matters even more, because you cannot spend money on
  advertising. Repeat customers and recommendations are the whole engine.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">The boring things that actually work</h2>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    <strong>Turn up when you said.</strong> A surprising number of the businesses
    you are competing with fail at this. Being the person who arrives on time is
    a genuine advantage and it costs nothing.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Say what you are doing before you are asked.</strong> "Running twenty
    minutes late" sent early is fine. The same information discovered by the
    customer is a problem.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Do slightly more than you promised, once.</strong> Not every time,
    because then it becomes the promise. Once, visibly, so they remember.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Make the next booking before you leave.</strong> "Same time in two
    weeks?" turns a customer into a regular in one sentence.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Set expectations you can actually hit</h2>

<p>
  Almost every unhappy customer is not unhappy about the work. They are unhappy
  because they expected something different. That is fixable in advance and
  nearly impossible to fix afterwards.
</p>

<p>
  So write down what is included and what is not, and say it before they pay.
  Say how long it takes and add some room. Under promise slightly and then
  deliver, rather than promising the best case and explaining yourself later.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">When it goes wrong</h2>

<p>
  It will. What separates businesses people recommend from businesses people warn
  about is entirely what happens in the next hour.
</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>Reply fast, even if the reply is only "I have seen this, give me an hour".</li>
  <li>Do not argue about who is right first. Ask what would make it right for them, then decide what you are willing to do.</li>
  <li>Fix it, redo it, or refund it. Decide your own rule for this in advance and put it in writing so you are not inventing a policy while upset.</li>
  <li>A customer whose problem was sorted out well often becomes more loyal than one who never had a problem. This is not a consolation, it is genuinely how it goes.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Keep records of what you agreed</h2>

<p>
  Agree changes over messages rather than in a doorway. Photograph finished work
  before you leave. Keep the receipts for what a job cost you.
</p>

<p>
  This sounds like admin and it is really protection. If somebody later disputes
  a payment with their card company, the money can be pulled back out and the
  burden is on your side to show the work was delivered as described. The
  evidence that settles that is the stuff you wrote down at the time, and it is
  impossible to reconstruct six months later.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <p class="m-0 text-sm">
    <strong>One rule that prevents most of this:</strong> take part of the money
    before you start on anything large, and do not hand over the finished thing
    before the payment has gone through. Deposits are normal. Every business you
    will ever hire takes one.
  </p>
</div>
